<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cnut the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 990–1035</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Canute the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cnut the Great was a Danish prince, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a>, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1016 and became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1018 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1028. Ruling this North Sea empire largely from England, he kept English law and institutions, used Danish and English earls and patronised the Church. His empire broke up soon after his death in 1035.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great.jpg" alt="Cnut the Great" width="296" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See Description (unknown) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canute_and_%C3%86lfgifu_cropped_(Canute).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sweyn’s raids on England culminated in its conquest in 1013, and his son <b>Cnut the Great</b> ruled England from 1016 and Denmark from about 1018, adding Norway in 1028.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Viking kings, the last of them Eric Bloodaxe, ruled York at intervals until 954, and under Æthelred the Unready renewed Danish attacks led to the conquest of England by Sweyn Forkbeard in 1013 and by his son <b>Cnut</b> in 1016.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Driven out by <b>Cnut the Great</b>, Olaf Haraldsson returned and was killed at the Battle of Stiklestad in 1030, and his rapid veneration as Saint Olaf gave the monarchy a sacred patron and a lasting focus of loyalty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A second wave of attacks from the 980s brought heavy tributes, the Danegeld, and the conquest of England by Sweyn Forkbeard in 1013; his son <b>Cnut the Great</b> ruled England, the Kingdom of Denmark and, from 1028, the Kingdom of Norway.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>And at Roskilde, at Christmas 1026, <b>Cnut the Great</b> had his regent and brother-in-law Ulf Jarl killed after a quarrel over chess.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings kept armed retinues, called out the ship levy (leiðangr) and spread the new faith, sometimes by force, and <b>Cnut the Great</b> ruled England, Denmark and Norway together.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaftesbury">Shaftesbury</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56,10&amp;year=1019" aria-label="Denmark on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4767 256 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cnut the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5217 526h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5217 526h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56,10&amp;year=1019">What was here in 1019? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
